WasmFS rename lock order: __syscall_renameat could deadlock against any path lookup. Everywhere else a directory is
locked before its child (Directory::Handle::getChild holds the parent while cacheChild locks the child it caches;
unlink/rmdir hold the parent while locking what they remove), but rename, holding both parents, walked newParent's
ancestors with curr->locked() (child, then parent), and locked oldParent before newParent even when newParent was its
ancestor. Seen in ikvmcraft (dotnet-runtime-jit findings R533): Minecraft's server thread renaming in its world
directory held "New World" and waited for "saves", while an IO thread held "saves" and waited for "New World"; the game
hung with every thread asleep. Now both parents' ancestor chains are collected before either is locked (one directory
lock at a time; renameMutex keeps them valid, since only a rename reparents a directory), the parent that is the other's
ancestor is locked first, and the ancestor test reads the collected chain. Adds a marker string so a deploy can prove the
patched library is linked.

--- a/system/lib/wasmfs/syscalls.cpp
+++ b/system/lib/wasmfs/syscalls.cpp
@@ -28,6 +28,8 @@
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <utility>
 #include <vector>
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <optional>
 #include <wasi/api.h>
 
 #include "backend.h"
@@ -38,6 +40,11 @@
 #include "socket_file.h"
 #include "special_files.h"
 #include "stream_file.h"
